Reclaim targets for partial preemption of elastic RayClusters, behind the
PartialPreemption feature gate (alpha, off by default), following the reactive
design of https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kueue/pull/17484.

A preemptor shrinks an admitted elastic Workload by writing the
kueue.x-k8s.io/reclaim-target-counts annotation. Nothing in this patch writes
it; with the gate off the annotation is ignored. With the gate on:

- The job reconciler calls the new JobWithPartialStop interface while a target
  is pending. RayCluster implements it by deleting the running workers above
  the target in whole ray.io/worker-group-replica-name groups, the group with
  the lowest controller.kubernetes.io/pod-deletion-cost first, then the newest.
  The RayCluster keeps its replicas, so KubeRay recreates the Pods, gated.
- The ElasticJobUngater ungates nothing while a target is pending, raises
  status.reclaimablePods to count - target once the running Pods are at or
  below the target, which releases their quota, and caps ungating at the target
  afterwards, so the recreated Pods stay gated.
- An in-place scale-down keeps a PodSet with a target at its spec count while
  the job's count is at or above the admitted count, since it is already
  charged the target. Below the admitted count it lowers the count and the
  target, then re-derives the reclaimable count after the spec update, which
  the Workload webhook accepts only then. The ungater retries that write.
- The Workload controller clears the reclaimable Pods of the shrunk PodSets and
  then the target, preemptor and requested-at annotations once the Workload no
  longer holds quota.

